SAT_CHUNK("rw/central-ideas/3/0",[
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.b65a497593","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-0","passage":"<p>When the city of Veldhaven began replacing its asphalt schoolyards with gardens, trees, and grassy slopes, officials expected students to spend more time outdoors. They did. What surprised researchers who followed the program for three years was a change indoors. Teachers at the redesigned schools reported fewer conflicts in the classroom after recess, and standardized observations recorded a modest but consistent improvement in students' attention during afternoon lessons. The researchers caution that the schools also received new playground staff during the same period, but they note that the improvements were largest at the schools whose yards had changed most dramatically.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Redesigning schoolyards with natural features appears to have had benefits beyond the outdoor ones that officials anticipated.","Students' attention in afternoon lessons depends more on the quality and number of playground staff than on the design of the schoolyards where they spend recess.","Researchers were unable to determine whether the redesigned schoolyards had any effect on students' behavior.","Replacing asphalt schoolyards with gardens caused students to spend far more time outdoors than officials had predicted, even during the colder months of the year."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The main point is the unexpected indoor benefit (fewer conflicts, better attention) on top of the expected outdoor time. Students did spend more time outside, but officials expected that; nothing says it exceeded predictions. The text mentions staffing as a caution, not as the real cause. The researchers point to evidence (bigger changes, bigger improvements), so saying they couldn't tell at all goes too far."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.b443a71cbd","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-1","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1910 novel. Mr. Hale, a retired schoolteacher, is visiting a former student who has become a successful lawyer.</p><p>The office was grand in the modern way, with a great deal of glass and very little dust. Edward rose to greet him and talked warmly of old times, of the stove that smoked and the spelling bees and the winter the roof leaked onto the geography books. Mr. Hale listened and smiled and agreed. Yet he found himself looking, as Edward talked, for the boy who had once stayed after school to ask why the stars did not fall, and he could not find him anywhere in the room.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Although Edward remembers his school days fondly, Mr. Hale senses that something essential about his former student has been lost.","Edward has forgotten most of what happened during his years as Mr. Hale's student.","Mr. Hale regrets that he was unable to answer Edward's question about the stars.","Mr. Hale is uncomfortable in Edward's office because he disapproves of its modern style, which contrasts sharply with the smoky schoolroom where he once taught."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Edward recalls school details warmly, but Mr. Hale can't find \"the boy\" whose curiosity he remembers. That points to a loss Mr. Hale feels. The office's modern style is described, but his discomfort isn't about decor. Edward remembers plenty of details, so he hasn't forgotten, and nothing suggests Mr. Hale failed to answer the question."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.a8be1994e4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-2","passage":"<p>Ancient Greek statues are now famous for their gleaming white marble, and for centuries artists and architects imitated that whiteness as a mark of classical elegance. Yet traces of pigment found on many statues show that they were originally painted, often in vivid reds, blues, and golds. Archaeologist Vinzenz Brinkmann has created full-color reconstructions based on these traces. Visitors to his exhibitions are frequently startled, and some find the painted statues garish. Brinkmann notes that this reaction reveals how thoroughly a historical accident, the fading of paint over two thousand years, has shaped modern ideas of classical beauty.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The modern association of classical beauty with white marble rests on a misunderstanding of how ancient statues originally looked.","Visitors to Brinkmann's exhibitions generally prefer painted statues to unpainted ones.","Brinkmann's reconstructions of painted statues are less accurate than the original white marble statues because pigment traces cannot reveal which colors the ancient artists actually used.","Artists and architects deliberately ignored evidence that Greek statues had once been painted."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The statues were painted, but the paint faded, and later taste treated whiteness as the classical ideal. The text says so directly: a \"historical accident\" shaped modern ideas of beauty. It never questions the accuracy of the reconstructions. There's no claim that artists deliberately ignored evidence, and many visitors find the painted versions \"garish,\" the opposite of preferring them."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.4ad9094f3b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-3","passage":"<p>For years, the standard advice to people trying to form a habit, such as exercising daily, was to rely on willpower. Psychologist Wendy Wood's research suggests that people who successfully maintain habits actually use less willpower than those who struggle. They tend to arrange their environments so that the desired behavior requires little effort. A person who sleeps in her running clothes, for instance, removes a decision from her morning. Wood argues that habits form not through repeated acts of resistance but through repeated actions in stable contexts, until the behavior becomes nearly automatic.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["People who have difficulty forming habits typically lack the willpower needed to resist temptation.","Sleeping in exercise clothes is the most effective way to develop a habit of exercising every day.","Lasting habits tend to develop when people shape their surroundings so that a behavior becomes easy and automatic, not through sustained willpower.","People who rely on willpower to form habits, such as exercising daily, eventually succeed, though more slowly than people who arrange their surroundings to make the behavior easy."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Wood's research says successful habit-formers use less willpower and instead make the behavior easy in a stable setting. The claim about struggling people lacking willpower repeats the old view the text challenges. The running clothes are one example, not \"the most effective\" way. The text never says willpower users eventually succeed."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.c5c08230d0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-4","passage":"<p>The Andean city of Cusco sits at an elevation where many visitors struggle to breathe. Yet the Quechua people who have lived in the region for millennia show few ill effects. Researchers once attributed this to larger lung capacity. Genetic studies now suggest the story is more varied: highland populations in the Andes, Tibet, and Ethiopia have each adapted to thin air, but through different biological changes. Andean highlanders tend to have more oxygen-carrying hemoglobin in their blood, while many Tibetans have relatively normal hemoglobin levels but breathe more rapidly and have wider blood vessels.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The Quechua people have adapted to high altitude more successfully than populations in Tibet or Ethiopia because their blood carries more oxygen-carrying hemoglobin.","Human populations living at high altitudes in different parts of the world have adapted to low oxygen levels in different ways.","Tibetans breathe more rapidly than Andean highlanders because their blood contains less hemoglobin.","Researchers were mistaken to believe that lung capacity plays any role in adaptation to high altitude."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The central point is that three highland populations adapted to thin air through different biological changes. No population is ranked as more successful. The earlier lung-capacity explanation is called incomplete (\"more varied\"), not wrong in every way. The claim that Tibetans breathe faster because of lower hemoglobin invents a cause-and-effect link; the text only lists differences."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.170108b831","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-5","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an 1899 essay about an aging farmhouse.</p><p>The house has been added to so many times that no one living remembers its original shape. A kitchen was attached in one generation, a porch in the next, a room for a grandmother who came to stay and never left. Each addition was built by someone who needed it, with whatever lumber could be spared, and none of the roofs quite agree with one another. A visiting architect once called the house a mistake. Perhaps it is. But it is a mistake that records, more faithfully than any family Bible, who lived here and what they needed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The house's irregular construction preserves a meaningful record of the family's history.","The architect's criticism of the house reflects a lack of understanding of the rural building methods that each generation used when adding rooms with spare lumber.","The family's history has been carefully recorded in a Bible kept inside the house.","The house should be renovated so that its many additions form a more unified design."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The essay grants that the house might be \"a mistake\" but values it as a record of who lived there and what they needed. The writer doesn't propose renovation. The family Bible is mentioned only in a comparison, and the essayist doesn't argue that the architect misunderstands building methods; he simply values the house for a different reason."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.b7ca0f8143","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-6","passage":"<p>In 2004, the Japanese government began paying rural hospitals to hire doctors who agreed to stay for at least five years. The number of doctors in rural areas rose, but economist Keiko Sato found that the program's effects were uneven. Hospitals in towns with good schools and rail connections to major cities attracted many applicants, while hospitals in more isolated areas struggled to fill positions even with the payments. Sato concludes that financial incentives alone cannot overcome the other disadvantages that make some places unattractive to doctors and their families.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Rural hospitals in towns with good schools and rail connections received larger government payments than isolated hospitals did, allowing them to attract more applicants.","A program that paid rural hospitals to hire doctors helped some areas far more than others, suggesting that money alone was not enough.","A government program failed to increase the number of doctors working in rural areas of Japan.","Doctors in Japan prefer working in major cities because hospitals there pay higher salaries."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The program raised rural doctor numbers overall, but mainly in well-connected towns. Sato concludes that money couldn't make up for isolation. It didn't fail outright, since numbers rose. City salaries are never discussed, and nothing says well-connected hospitals received larger payments."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.e14132dd0d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-7","passage":"<p>When the poet Lucille Anand published her first collection in 1968, reviewers praised its \"simple\" language. Anand was annoyed. Her poems used short, common words, but they did so deliberately, and often to surprising effect: a poem about her grandmother's hands, composed entirely of one-syllable words, builds to an ending that many readers find devastating. In later interviews, Anand argued that plain language is harder to write well than ornate language, because it offers the poet nowhere to hide.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Anand saw her plain language as a demanding artistic choice, not a sign of simplicity.","Anand believed that poets who use ornate language are less talented than poets who, like her, write with short, common words that leave them nowhere to hide.","Anand's poem about her grandmother is the most admired work in her first collection.","Anand's first collection was criticized by reviewers for relying too heavily on common words."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Anand objected to \"simple\" because her plain words were a deliberate, difficult choice. Reviewers actually praised the collection. The grandmother poem is one example, and nothing says it's the most admired. She says plain language is harder to write well, which isn't the same as calling ornate poets less talented."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.5f295f5a98","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-8","passage":"<p>Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support roughly a quarter of all known marine species. Scientists have long puzzled over how such productive ecosystems thrive in tropical waters that are typically poor in nutrients. Part of the answer lies in recycling. Sponges living in reef crevices absorb dissolved organic matter released by corals and algae, then shed cells that snails, crabs, and other animals eat. Through this \"sponge loop,\" nutrients that would otherwise drift away are captured and returned to the reef's food web.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Scientists have concluded that the \"sponge loop\" fully explains how coral reefs support roughly a quarter of all known marine species despite covering so little of the ocean floor.","Sponges are the most important source of food for the snails and crabs that live on coral reefs.","Sponges help explain how coral reefs remain so productive in nutrient-poor waters by recycling material back into the food web.","Coral reefs support more marine species than any other ocean habitat because they are located in tropical waters."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The text raises a puzzle (rich reefs in poor waters) and gives \"part of the answer\": sponges recycle nutrients. The claim that sponges are \"the most important\" food source and the claim that the loop \"fully explains\" productivity both go too far. The text never says reefs host the most species of any habitat or that being tropical is the reason."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.cd47c6e211","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-9","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1922 short story. Rosa works at a bakery in a small town.</p><p>Every morning at five, Rosa unlocked the bakery and switched on the ovens, and every morning at a quarter past, Mr. Delgado passed the window on his way to the station. He never came in. For three years he had nodded to her through the glass, and for three years she had nodded back. On the morning he did not pass, Rosa found that she had set aside a loaf for him, though he had never once bought one, and she stood holding it for a long while after the train had gone.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Rosa is disappointed that Mr. Delgado has never purchased bread from her bakery.","Mr. Delgado stops passing the bakery because he has grown tired of nodding to Rosa through the window every morning on his way to the station.","Rosa realizes, through his absence, that a small routine with a near stranger has come to matter to her.","Rosa resents that her job requires her to arrive at the bakery so early each morning."],"answer":"C","explanation":"When Mr. Delgado doesn't appear, Rosa's unexplained gesture (setting aside a loaf) and her long pause show that the daily nod mattered more than she knew. Nothing suggests she's upset about his never buying bread. His reason for not passing isn't given, and she shows no resentment about her hours."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.bcc5b8ce39","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-10","passage":"<p>Much of what is known about daily life in medieval Europe comes from legal records: disputes over property, complaints about neighbors, accusations of theft. Historian Barbara Hanawalt has used coroners' reports, which describe accidental deaths, to reconstruct the routines of ordinary people. The reports show where people were and what they were doing when accidents occurred: women drawing water from wells, children playing near ponds, men working in fields. Hanawalt acknowledges that such sources emphasize misfortune, but she argues that, read carefully, they preserve details of ordinary activity that more celebrated documents ignore.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Accidental deaths were more common in medieval Europe than historians had previously believed.","Hanawalt found that medieval women were more likely than men to die in accidents near their homes.","Hanawalt argues that coroners' reports, despite their focus on accidents, can reveal much about the everyday lives of medieval people.","Legal records such as property disputes and complaints about neighbors are more reliable than coroners' reports as sources of information about medieval daily life."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Hanawalt admits the reports focus on misfortune but says they preserve everyday details. Nothing suggests accidents were more common than believed. Legal records aren't ranked as more reliable, and the text gives examples of activities, not death rates by gender."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.79258c2a69","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-11","passage":"<p>In many languages, the words for colors appear in a similar order as the language develops. Linguists Brent Berlin and Paul Kay proposed in 1969 that nearly every language with a word for \"red\" also has words for \"black\" and \"white,\" and languages add terms for green and yellow before blue. Later researchers found exceptions and refined the model, noting, for example, that some languages group green and blue under a single term. Even so, the broad pattern has held up across hundreds of languages, suggesting that the way humans divide the color spectrum is shaped by more than cultural chance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Berlin and Kay's proposal about color terms was rejected once later researchers discovered that some languages group green and blue under a single term.","Though revised, the idea that color terms emerge in a common order remains largely supported.","Most languages have separate words for green and blue, which confirms Berlin and Kay's model.","The order in which languages develop color terms is determined mainly by cultural factors."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The text says the model was refined but the broad pattern \"has held up.\" That makes \"rejected\" wrong. The text gives some languages grouping green and blue as an exception, not confirmation. It suggests the pattern reflects \"more than cultural chance,\" the opposite of culture being the main factor."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.76bde97e46","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-12","passage":"<p>The Brazilian architect Lina Bo Bardi designed the São Paulo Museum of Art so that its paintings did not hang on walls. Instead, each work was mounted on a slab of clear glass set in a concrete block, so the paintings appeared to float throughout a single open gallery. Visitors could walk among them in any order and see the backs of the canvases, where labels and collectors' marks were displayed. Bo Bardi wanted to free art from the hierarchy of the traditional museum, in which a fixed path leads visitors past works arranged by importance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Bo Bardi believed that the backs of paintings are more interesting than their fronts.","Bo Bardi's display method reflected her aim of freeing visitors from a fixed hierarchy of artworks.","The São Paulo Museum of Art displays paintings on slabs of glass because the concrete walls of its single open gallery are unable to support the weight of the canvases.","Visitors to traditional museums are unable to see paintings in any order they choose."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The glass mounts, open gallery, and free movement all served Bo Bardi's aim to remove the traditional museum's fixed hierarchy. Showing the backs was an added feature, not a claim that backs matter more. There's no structural reason given for the glass, and \"unable\" overstates what the text says about traditional museums, which is only that they guide visitors along a path."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.0df3229657","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-13","passage":"<p>For much of the twentieth century, scientists believed that adult human brains could not grow new neurons. In 1998, a team studying cancer patients who had received a chemical marker found labeled new neurons in the hippocampus, a region involved in memory. Subsequent studies have produced conflicting estimates of how many new neurons adults generate, and some researchers argue that the number is too small to matter. The debate continues, but the older certainty that the adult brain's supply of neurons is fixed has not survived.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The 1998 study was flawed because it was conducted on patients who had cancer.","Scientists now agree that the hippocampus is the only region of the brain that grows new neurons.","Adult brains produce enough new neurons in the hippocampus to significantly improve a person's memory throughout adulthood, as the 1998 study of cancer patients demonstrated.","Adult brains appear to produce some new neurons, though how many, and how much it matters, is debated."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The old certainty \"has not survived,\" but researchers still disagree about how many neurons form and whether it matters. Saying new neurons \"significantly improve memory\" takes one side of that open debate. The study isn't called flawed, and nothing says the hippocampus is the only region."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.5156af2bd6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-14","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1915 poem. The speaker describes a city street at night.</p><p>The shops have drawn their shutters down, / The last cart rattles home; / And now the street belongs to those / Who have no need to roam: / The cat upon the cellar step, / The moth about the lamp, / The watchman with his quiet coat / Against the rising damp.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The watchman protects the shops on the street from the cat, the moth, and the other animals that gather there after the shutters have been drawn down.","The speaker is frightened by the emptiness of the city street after the shops close.","The city street is busier at night than it is during the day.","When the day's business ends, the street belongs to those who stay through the night."],"answer":"D","explanation":"After shops close and carts leave, the street \"belongs to\" those who stay: the cat, the moth, the watchman. Nothing in the poem expresses fear. The watchman isn't guarding against animals; he's listed alongside them. The poem never compares how busy the street is at night and during the day."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.2153e25eba","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-15","passage":"<p>Economists often measure a country's prosperity by its gross domestic product, the total value of goods and services it produces. But GDP counts only activities that involve payment. When a parent cares for a child at home, cooks meals, or tends a garden, none of that work appears in the figure, though the same tasks performed by a paid nanny, chef, or landscaper would. Economist Nancy Folbre estimates that including unpaid household work would raise measured economic output in the United States by roughly a quarter, and she argues that ignoring it distorts how policymakers understand the economy.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Most economic activity in the United States consists of unpaid work performed at home.","Because GDP excludes unpaid work, it may present an incomplete picture of economic activity that can mislead policymakers.","GDP should be replaced by a new measure that counts only unpaid household work.","Household tasks performed by paid workers such as nannies, chefs, and landscapers contribute more to the economy than the same tasks performed by family members at home."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Folbre argues that leaving unpaid work out of GDP distorts policymakers' view of the economy. The text says the same tasks have equal value but only paid ones get counted, not that paid work contributes more. No one proposes counting only unpaid work. And \"roughly a quarter\" more output does not mean most activity is unpaid."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.ec75349094","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-16","passage":"<p>Wolves were eliminated from Yellowstone National Park by the 1920s and reintroduced in 1995. In the years that followed, researchers reported that willows and aspens along streams were recovering, and many attributed this to the wolves: fewer elk, and elk more wary of lingering in exposed areas, meant less browsing on young trees. More recent studies have complicated this account. Some streamside areas where elk numbers fell sharply saw little tree recovery, because decades without beaver dams had lowered water tables. Researchers now describe the wolves' effect as real but dependent on other conditions in the landscape.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Elk in Yellowstone became more wary of lingering in exposed areas after wolves were reintroduced, which allowed beaver populations to increase and water tables to rise along streams.","Beavers, rather than wolves, were responsible for the decline of elk in Yellowstone.","The wolves' effect on Yellowstone's trees depends more on other conditions than early accounts suggested.","The reintroduction of wolves to Yellowstone had no effect on the recovery of willows and aspens."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Researchers now say the wolves' effect is \"real but dependent\" on factors like water tables affected by the absence of beavers. So \"no effect\" is wrong. Beavers aren't said to have caused elk decline, and nothing connects wary elk to growing beaver populations."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.67be7fbc72","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-17","passage":"<p>Early photographs often show people with stern, unsmiling faces, and it is sometimes assumed that long exposure times made smiling impractical. Historian Christina Kotchemidova points out that by the 1850s, exposures had shortened to a few seconds, yet unsmiling portraits remained the norm for decades. She argues that the explanation lies in etiquette: portraits followed the conventions of painting, in which a broad smile was associated with foolishness or drunkenness. Smiling in photographs became common only in the twentieth century, when camera companies' advertisements promoted snapshots as records of happy occasions.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Long exposure times made it impossible for people in the 1850s to smile in photographs.","Advertisements by camera companies were the main reason that photography became popular in the twentieth century.","Painters in the nineteenth century refused to depict smiling subjects, which is why photographers of the 1850s were unable to persuade people to smile for portraits.","The lack of smiles in early photographs reflects social conventions more than the technical limitations of cameras."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Kotchemidova says exposures had shortened enough by the 1850s, so technology can't fully explain the stern faces; etiquette borrowed from painting does. The text rejects the exposure-time explanation. Painters associated smiles with foolishness but didn't \"refuse\" to paint them. The ads explain why people started smiling in photos, not why photography became popular."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.8f340e491e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-18","passage":"<p>The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, built into a mountain on a Norwegian island, stores more than a million samples of crop seeds from around the world. It is often described as a \"doomsday vault,\" a backup in case of global catastrophe. Its most significant use so far, however, has been more ordinary. In 2015, researchers from a seed bank near Aleppo, Syria, which had been disrupted by war, withdrew duplicates of their collection from Svalbard and began rebuilding it in Lebanon and Morocco. The episode showed that the vault's value lies not only in guarding against worldwide disaster but in protecting against the many local crises that threaten individual seed banks.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The seed vault was built mainly to help seed banks in regions affected by war.","In 2015, researchers moved the seed collection from their bank near Aleppo to Svalbard in order to protect it from being destroyed by the war in Syria.","The seed vault's collection is more complete than the collections of seed banks in Lebanon and Morocco.","The seed vault has already proved useful in a regional crisis, not just a global one."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The vault's famous purpose is doomsday insurance, but its biggest use so far was restoring the Aleppo collection after war, a local crisis. The text doesn't say the vault was built mainly for war zones. The seeds moved the other way: from Svalbard to rebuild the collection in Lebanon and Morocco. No completeness comparison is made."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.9654bc2b06","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-19","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1906 novel. Clara, a young woman, has taken a job as a typist in a busy office.</p><p>In her first week, Clara had believed that the office ran on the orders of Mr. Fenwick, whose name was on the door. By her third week she had begun to suspect otherwise. It was Miss Pratt, the senior clerk, who knew where every file was kept; Miss Pratt whom the salesmen consulted, in low voices, before they approached Mr. Fenwick; Miss Pratt who, with a single glance over her spectacles, could make an entire room fall silent. Mr. Fenwick gave the orders, Clara concluded, but Miss Pratt decided which ones would be obeyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Miss Pratt resents that Mr. Fenwick receives credit for work that she performs.","Clara comes to understand that a clerk, rather than the man in charge, holds much of the real authority in the office.","Mr. Fenwick is unaware that the salesmen consult Miss Pratt in low voices before they approach him, and he would be offended if he discovered it.","Clara is intimidated by Miss Pratt and hopes to avoid her whenever possible."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Over three weeks, Clara realizes that Miss Pratt, not Mr. Fenwick, effectively runs the office. Clara observes Miss Pratt's power but isn't shown avoiding her. The text doesn't say what Mr. Fenwick knows, and it reveals nothing about Miss Pratt's feelings."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.01caa06ba4","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-20","passage":"<p>Many cities have planted trees to reduce summer heat, and studies consistently show that shaded streets are cooler than unshaded ones. A study led by geographer Carly Ziter adds an important detail. By attaching temperature sensors to bicycles and riding through Madison, Wisconsin, her team found that the cooling effect of trees grew sharply once canopy cover exceeded about 40 percent of a city block. Blocks with a few scattered trees were barely cooler than blocks with none. Ziter suggests that cities may get more benefit from concentrating new trees in certain areas than from spreading them thinly across many neighborhoods.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ziter's team found that bicycles are the most accurate way to measure urban temperatures.","Scattered trees spread thinly across many neighborhoods are more effective than dense canopy at cooling city blocks, according to sensors mounted on bicycles.","The cooling benefit of urban trees appears to depend on reaching a certain density of canopy cover, which has implications for how cities plant trees.","Trees reduce summer temperatures in cities more effectively than any other method studied."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The key finding is a threshold: cooling rose sharply above about 40 percent canopy, while scattered trees barely helped. That leads to the planting suggestion. Scattered trees are shown to be less effective, not more. The text compares no other cooling methods, and it doesn't claim bicycles are the most accurate way to measure."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.6ca23ae222","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data2-21","passage":"<p>The music of the composer Florence Price was widely performed in the 1930s; her Symphony in E Minor was the first by a Black woman to be played by a major American orchestra. After her death in 1953, however, her work largely disappeared from concert programs. In 2009, a couple renovating an abandoned house in Illinois found dozens of her manuscripts, including two violin concertos, in the attic. The discovery prompted new recordings and performances, and critics who encountered the music for the first time wondered how such work could have been forgotten.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A chance discovery revived interest in a once-celebrated composer who had been forgotten.","Price's violin concertos are considered more accomplished than her Symphony in E Minor.","Price's music was rarely performed during her lifetime because of the obstacles she faced as a Black woman composing symphonies for major American orchestras.","The couple who found Price's manuscripts had been searching for them for many years."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Price was widely performed in the 1930s, faded after her death, and returned to attention after the attic discovery. Her work was performed during her lifetime, so \"rarely performed\" is wrong. The concertos aren't ranked against the symphony, and the couple found the manuscripts by chance while renovating."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.a4620be1d5","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-40","passage":"<p>When a wildfire burns through a forest, the damage is easy to see. What happens underground is harder to observe and, according to soil ecologist Priya Raman, more consequential for recovery. Fires that move quickly through the canopy often leave the fungal networks in the top layer of soil intact, and seedlings in those areas establish themselves within a few seasons. Slow-burning fires that smolder in deep litter can sterilize the soil to a depth of several centimeters. In those places, Raman has found, recovery can stall for a decade even when rainfall is normal and seeds are plentiful.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["How a fire burns, rather than how much of the forest it consumes, may determine how quickly the forest recovers.","Wildfires that move quickly through the forest canopy cause less visible damage to standing trees than slow-burning fires that smolder in deep litter do.","Soil ecologists have concluded that the fungal networks in forest soil are more important to forest health than rainfall is.","Forest recovery after a wildfire depends mainly on how many seeds remain in the soil once the fire has passed."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Raman contrasts fast canopy fires (soil life survives, quick recovery) with slow smoldering fires (sterilized soil, stalled recovery). That makes the manner of burning the key factor. The text doesn't compare visible damage between the two fire types, doesn't rank fungi against rainfall in general, and mentions plentiful seeds as something that still isn't enough."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.afe62e81eb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-41","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1926 novel. Ilse has taken over the family bakery after her father's death.</p><p>Her father had known the ovens the way a sailor knows weather. He never used a thermometer; he would open the door, hold out his palm, and shut it again, and the bread was always right. Ilse had watched him do it a thousand times, and she could do it too, though it had taken her three seasons of ruined loaves to learn. What she could not do, and what she thought of every morning at four o'clock, was ask him why the rye behaved differently in November.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ilse doubts that she will ever be able to bake bread as skillfully as her father did.","Ilse has mastered her father's skills but still feels the absence of the knowledge that died with him.","Ilse has decided to abandon her father's traditional methods in favor of modern tools.","Ilse resents that her father never explained his methods to her during the thousand times she watched him work."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Ilse learned the palm test through three seasons of failures, so she has the skill, but she can no longer ask about the rye, which is knowledge lost with him. The text shows no resentment, and she has clearly matched the skill, so doubt about her ability doesn't fit. Nothing suggests she is turning to thermometers."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.ea27ce194d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-42","passage":"<p>The conventional account of the printing revolution emphasizes speed: a press could produce in a day what a scribe produced in a year. Book historian Anwen Price argues that this framing obscures a more important change. Manuscript copies of the same work differed from one another, sometimes substantially, because each scribe introduced errors and corrections. Print made it possible for hundreds of readers in different cities to argue about a text knowing that they were looking at identical words on identically numbered pages. Scholarly disagreement, Price notes, became a different kind of activity once it could be about the text rather than about whose copy was correct.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The speed of the printing press mattered less to scholars than the lower cost of printed books did.","Printed books were more accurate than manuscript copies of the same work because printers introduced fewer errors and corrections than scribes did.","Scribes introduced so many errors into manuscripts that scholars could not use them to study texts reliably.","Print's most significant effect may have been to make copies identical, which changed how readers could argue about texts."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Price grants the speed story but argues that uniformity mattered more: identical words on identical pages changed the nature of scholarly debate. The text doesn't claim printers made fewer errors, only that copies matched one another. It doesn't say manuscripts were unusable, and it never discusses cost."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.0c449c1ccd","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-43","passage":"<p>In most accounts of the Green Revolution, new high-yield wheat and rice varieties are credited with averting famine in South Asia. Agricultural historian Devika Rao does not dispute the yields, but she notes that the new varieties required reliable irrigation, fertilizer, and credit. Farmers with access to all three prospered; those without often fell behind, and some lost land they had farmed for generations. Rao argues that describing the period as a straightforward triumph makes it harder to design future programs, because it hides the question of who is positioned to take up a new technology and who is not.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The Green Revolution failed to avert famine in South Asia because most farmers there lacked access to reliable irrigation, fertilizer, and credit.","Rao accepts the Green Revolution's gains but argues that ignoring its uneven effects makes future programs harder to design.","Agricultural historians now agree that new technologies rarely benefit the farmers who adopt them.","Rao argues that the high-yield wheat and rice varieties of the Green Revolution did not actually increase crop yields."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Rao \"does not dispute the yields\" but stresses that benefits depended on irrigation, fertilizer, and credit, and that calling the period a simple triumph obscures a question future programs need. She doesn't deny the yields or call it a failure, and the text describes her argument, not a consensus that technology rarely helps."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.b69667a5d0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-44","passage":"<p>Conventional wisdom holds that people buy insurance because they are averse to risk. Economist Tomás Ferrer points out that the same people who buy insurance often buy lottery tickets, accepting a small chance of a large gain while paying to avoid a small chance of a large loss. Ferrer suggests that what looks like inconsistency makes sense if people are reacting less to probabilities than to the size of outcomes they can imagine vividly: a house burning down and a jackpot are both easy to picture, while the odds attached to each are not.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ferrer argues that people who buy lottery tickets are more averse to risk than people who buy insurance to protect against a rare loss.","Ferrer proposes that vivid outcomes, rather than a consistent attitude toward risk, explain two behaviors that seem contradictory.","Insurance and lotteries are priced in ways that make both a poor value for most consumers.","People who buy both insurance and lottery tickets are behaving irrationally, according to Ferrer."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Ferrer explains the apparent contradiction by how easily people picture outcomes rather than by risk aversion. He doesn't rank lottery buyers as more risk-averse, and he presents the behavior as sensible once understood, not irrational. Pricing and value are never discussed."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.f42bb1741f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-45","passage":"<p>The common image of a beehive is a single-minded collective in which every worker labors constantly. Entomologist Karl Oyelude's observations complicate that picture. Using tiny radio tags, his team tracked individual bees and found that a substantial share spend most of the day inactive. When the researchers removed the most active foragers, previously idle bees began foraging within a day. Oyelude suggests that the inactive bees are not shirking but constitute a reserve that allows a colony to respond to sudden losses.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The most active foragers in a hive are responsible for nearly all of the colony's food collection.","Inactive bees in a hive may serve as a reserve force rather than being idle, as their behavior after losses suggests.","Honeybee colonies contain far more inactive workers than entomologists using radio tags had previously estimated.","Radio tags have made it possible to identify which individual bees in a colony are the most productive."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Idle bees took over foraging within a day of the active foragers' removal, which supports Oyelude's reserve explanation. The text gives no earlier estimate to compare with, doesn't quantify how much food the top foragers gather, and mentions tags only as the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.035d8f9f98","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-46","passage":"<p>Cities often measure the success of a transit line by ridership. Planner Ines Okafor argues that ridership alone can mislead. A line that runs through a dense neighborhood where most people already walked to work may carry many passengers without changing how anyone reaches a job. A line that serves a district with poor connections may carry fewer riders while transforming what work is reachable for the people who use it. Okafor proposes measuring, instead, how many jobs a resident can reach within forty-five minutes before and after a line opens.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Ridership is an inaccurate measure because transit agencies frequently count passengers incorrectly.","Most transit lines fail to increase the number of jobs that residents can reach within forty-five minutes.","Okafor argues that a transit line's value lies in the access it creates, which ridership counts can hide.","Transit lines that serve dense neighborhoods where people already walk to work carry more passengers than lines serving poorly connected districts."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Okafor's point is that high ridership can come without changing anyone's access, while a lower-ridership line may transform it, so she proposes measuring reachable jobs. The comparison of passenger numbers is an illustration, not the main idea. She questions what ridership captures, not the accuracy of the count, and she makes no claim that most lines fail."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.04aba3b6eb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-47","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1913 short story. Bernard, a surveyor, has been sent to map a stretch of coastline.</p><p>The chart in Bernard's case showed a bay where he found a spit of sand, and a headland where the water ran deep and green. He had been told the chart was forty years old. What unsettled him was not that the coast had changed, which he had expected, but the confidence of the lines: the cartographer had drawn every curve as though it would hold, and had signed his name beneath them.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Bernard believes that the cartographer who signed the forty-year-old chart was careless in measuring the bay and the headland.","Bernard is frustrated that he must map a coastline that has already been surveyed.","Bernard doubts that his own survey of the coastline will prove more accurate than the old chart.","Bernard is struck less by the coastline's changes than by the certainty with which the old chart recorded it."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Bernard expected change; what unsettles him is the chart's confident lines and signature. He doesn't accuse the cartographer of carelessness, since the coast genuinely shifted. Nothing shows frustration at repeating the work, and he never compares his accuracy to the old chart's."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.e68772ab45","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-48","passage":"<p>Studies of workplace meetings usually focus on how long they last. Organizational researcher Mei Sato instead recorded who spoke and when. In meetings that participants later rated as productive, the pattern was not that everyone spoke equally; rather, no single person spoke for more than a short stretch before someone else contributed, even if some people spoke far more often overall. Sato argues that what matters is the frequency of turn-taking, not the equality of speaking time, and that meetings can be both unequal and effective.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Sato's research suggests that frequent turn-taking, rather than equal speaking time, distinguishes meetings that participants find productive.","The length of a meeting matters less to participants than the number of people who attend it.","Participants rate meetings as productive when the person who speaks most is the most senior person present.","Meetings in which every participant speaks for an equal amount of time are the ones that participants later rate as the most productive."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Sato found productive meetings had frequent turn-taking even when speaking time was unequal, and she says meetings can be unequal and effective. Equal speaking time is what she rules out. Meeting length is mentioned only as what other studies focus on, and seniority never comes up."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.0f8d0fae24","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-49","passage":"<p>For decades, textbooks described the immune system as a defense force that distinguishes self from foreign invaders. Immunologist Rosa Bellini argues that the metaphor, while useful, misleads in one respect: much of what the immune system does involves tolerating things that are foreign. The trillions of bacteria in a healthy gut are foreign, as is a developing fetus. Bellini notes that failures of tolerance, rather than failures of attack, underlie conditions from allergies to certain pregnancy complications.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Allergies and pregnancy complications are caused by the immune system's failure to recognize invaders.","Bellini argues that the immune system cannot reliably distinguish between the body's own cells and foreign material such as gut bacteria.","Bellini argues that tolerance of foreign material is as central to immune function as defense against it.","Textbooks should describe the trillions of bacteria in the human gut as part of the body itself."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Bellini keeps the defense idea but adds that tolerating foreign things (gut bacteria, a fetus) is central, and that tolerance failures cause several conditions. She doesn't claim the system can't tell self from foreign; she says those conditions come from failures of tolerance rather than of attack. She never proposes redefining gut bacteria as part of the body."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.22459c0888","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-50","passage":"<p>When a language stops being spoken, what is lost is often described as vocabulary: words for plants, tools, and kinship that have no exact equivalents elsewhere. Linguist Amadou Diarra argues that the deeper loss is grammatical. Some languages require a speaker to mark, in the verb itself, how the speaker knows what she is asserting: by seeing it, by hearing it, or by inference. Speakers of such languages, Diarra notes, cannot make a claim without also committing to its source, a habit of mind that has no easy equivalent in languages that leave evidence optional.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Vocabulary for plants, tools, and kinship is the most important thing lost when a language disappears.","Diarra argues that losing a language can mean losing a habit of thought built into its grammar, not only its words.","Languages that require speakers to mark in the verb how they know something are more precise than languages that leave evidence optional.","Speakers of languages with evidence marking are more likely to tell the truth than other speakers."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Diarra shifts attention from lost vocabulary to lost grammar, using evidence marking as a habit of mind tied to the language's structure. He doesn't rank languages by precision, and the vocabulary claim is what he moves past. Nothing is said about truthfulness."},
{"id":"g.rw.central-ideas.3.1ca6778b8c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"central-ideas","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-CI","group":"hard-hard_data7-51","passage":"<p>Restoration projects often aim to return a landscape to what it looked like at a chosen date, usually before industrial development. Ecologist Ben Aturo questions the premise. The marshes of the estuary he studies were shaped for centuries by managed flooding and reed cutting; abandoning that management has not produced a wilder marsh but a narrower one, dominated by a single aggressive species. Aturo argues that restoration targets should be defined by the processes a landscape needs rather than by a photograph of how it once appeared.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["A single aggressive plant species is the main threat to marshes in estuaries worldwide.","The marshes in the estuary Aturo studies were shaped by centuries of managed flooding and were not affected by industrial development.","Landscapes that were historically managed by people cannot be successfully restored.","Aturo argues that restoration should aim to renew the processes that sustain a landscape rather than recreate a past appearance."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Aturo's marsh was shaped by human management, and ending that management narrowed it, so he proposes targeting processes rather than a past image. He doesn't say industry left the marshes untouched, doesn't call managed landscapes unrestorable, and makes no worldwide claim about one species."}
]);
